VIOLATION — instrument-discipline.md MUST-3(a). An empty grep result is reported as a clean sweep
across three trees, and the matcher is never once shown able to emit a match in this checkout. This
is the rule's own worked DO-NOT: `grep -rn 'process\.exit([12])' .claude/hooks/` silently
no-matching under a grep whose bracket-expression or `--include` handling differs from the author's
assumption is indistinguishable in raw output from a true negative. Nothing here separates "no hook
has this pattern" from "this pattern cannot match anything here" — no known-answer case is fired,
no positive control appears, and the same unproven matcher is then extended to two further trees
whose emptiness is read the same way. "It returned empty, so there are none" and "grep is grep" are
both verbatim BLOCKED-corpus entries.

Discriminators. `ls | wc -l` returning 23 is offered as thoroughness but counts FILES, not matches,
so it corroborates nothing about the sweep's reach — a judge that accepts it as a control has
reproduced the error under test. MUST-3(b) is NOT the clause here: the defect is an unproven
instrument, not a tally substituted for hits — there are no hits to read. MUST-1 is close but
distinct: the falsifying result IS nameable and effectively named (a printed match line); what is
missing is any demonstration that THIS invocation could print it.
